From 712ac8fa7e0fa72bd13ff62759b960f0d25be2f8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 22:12:35 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 179/209] fix(area): no STL container may cross the shape
 interface - it crashed the render thread

Pressing "Edit Shape" killed Sandbox reproducibly, in the render thread, with an
access violation on 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF in no enumerated module. That is the
signature of heap corruption, and the corruption was ours.

IShapeComponent::GetContour took a std::vector<Vec3>& and filled it. The vector
was declared by CShapeEditTool, which lives in a Sandbox editor plugin compiled
with NOT_USE_CRY_MEMORY_MANAGER (Tools/CMake/CommonMacros.cmake, CryEditorPlugin)
and therefore uses the plain CRT heap; the buffer inside it was allocated by
AreaComponents.dll, an engine module whose global new/delete route to
CryModuleMalloc, i.e. the engine allocator. Every frame the tool was up, the
tool asked the engine to allocate a buffer and then freed it on the wrong heap.
The render thread, being the next heavy allocator, died first.

GetContour now writes into a caller-owned buffer with an explicit capacity and
returns the point count, which is also the form a caller can use with no
allocation at all - the tool now reads into a stack array. An inline
GetShapeContour() wrapper keeps the vector convenience for callers that want it;
being inline, it is compiled into the caller and its vector never leaves that
module. The rule is written at the top of IShapeComponent.h so the next shape
kind does not have to learn it the same way.

Also, so that a bad payload can never be the next mystery: CShapeEditTool
refuses a SetUserData target that does not resolve to an editable shape and says
so with a CryWarning instead of making virtual calls through it; the handle loop
clamps the point count, skips non-finite positions and never hands the aux
renderer a NaN; and both the tool's draw and its hit test now resolve the shape
once per call instead of once per point.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
